{"date": "2026-11-21", "post_id": 1513943, "title": "Voyager's Neptune", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-21-voyagers-neptune/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Cruising through the outer solar system, the Voyager 2 spacecraft made its closest <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/history/30-years-ago-voyager-2-explores-neptune/\">approach to Neptune</a> on August 25, 1989, the only spacecraft to visit the most distant <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_giant\">ice giant planet</a>. Based on the images recorded during its close encounter and in the following days, <a href=\"https://www.rolfolsenastrophotography.com/Astrophotography/Solar-System/i-vjMHSxz\">this inspired composited scene</a> covers the dim outer planet, largest <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-23-flying-past-neptunes-moon-triton/\">moon Triton</a>, and faint system of rings. From just beyond Neptune's orbit, the interplanetary perspective looks back toward the Sun, capturing the planet and Triton as thin <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=130414\">sunlit crescents</a>. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=100808\">Cirrus clouds</a> and a dark band circle Neptune's south polar region, with a cloudy vortex above the pole <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/1003.3240\">itself</a>. Parts of the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rings_of_Neptune\">very faint ring system</a> along with the three bright ring arcs were first imaged by Voyager during the fly-by, though the faintest segments are modeled in this composited picture. Spanning 7.5 degrees, the background starfield is composed from sky survey data centered on the constellation Camelopardalis, corresponding to the outbound Voyager's view of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/\">magnificent Neptunian system</a>. Still cruising in 2026, the Voyager 2 spacecraft is <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/where-are-voyager-1-and-voyager-2-now/\">now in interstellar space</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>Sunday's Childe", "credit": "Assembly/Processing - <a href=\"http://www.rolfolsenastrophotography.com/\">Rolf Olsen</a>,<br>Data - <a href=\"http://voyager.jpl.nasa.gov/\">Voyager 2</a>, <a href=\"http://pds-rings.seti.org/voyager/iss/raw_images.html\">NASA Planetary Data System</a>", "copyright": "Assembly/Processing - <a href=\"http://www.rolfolsenastrophotography.com/\">Rolf Olsen</a>,<br>Data - <a href=\"http://voyager.jpl.nasa.gov/\">Voyager 2</a>, <a href=\"http://pds-rings.seti.org/voyager/iss/raw_images.html\">NASA Planetary Data System</a>", "alt": "Composite Voyager image of Neptune in a crescent phase.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-21-voyagers-neptune/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Neptune-South-Pole-Voyager-2_2327x1670.jpg?w=2327&h=1670&fit=clip", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 21 - Voyager&#039;s Neptune</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-21-voyagers-neptune/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Cruising through the outer solar system, the Voyager 2 spacecraft made its closest approach to Neptune on August 25, 1989, the only spacecraft to visit the most distant ice giant planet. Based on the images recorded during its close encounter and in the following days, this inspired composited scene covers the dim outer planet, largest moon Triton, and faint system of rings. From just beyond Neptune&#039;s orbit, the interplanetary perspective looks back toward the Sun, capturing the planet and Triton as thin sunlit crescents. Cirrus clouds and a dark band circle Neptune&#039;s south polar region, with a cloudy vortex above the pole itself. Parts of the very faint ring system along with the three bright ring arcs were first imaged by Voyager during the fly-by, though the faintest segments are modeled in this composited picture. Spanning 7.5 degrees, the background starfield is composed from sky survey data centered on the constellation Camelopardalis, corresponding to the outbound Voyager&#039;s view of the magnificent Neptunian system. Still cruising in 2026, the Voyager 2 spacecraft is now in interstellar space.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: Sunday&#039;s Childe\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"voyager&#039;s neptune\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Voyager&#039;s Neptune\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-21-voyagers-neptune/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Neptune-South-Pole-Voyager-2_2327x1670.jpg?w=2327&#038;h=1670&#038;fit=clip\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Neptune-South-Pole-Voyager-2_2327x1670.jpg?w=2327&#038;h=1670&#038;fit=clip\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Composite Voyager image of Neptune in a crescent phase.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-21T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-20T11:44:22-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Voyager&#039;s Neptune\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Neptune-South-Pole-Voyager-2_2327x1670.jpg?w=2327&#038;h=1670&#038;fit=clip\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 21<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Neptune-South-Pole-Voyager-2_2327x1670.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Neptune-South-Pole-Voyager-2_2327x1670.jpg\" alt=\"Composite Voyager image of Neptune in a crescent phase.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Voyager&#039;s Neptune </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\nAssembly/Processing - <a href=\"http://www.rolfolsenastrophotography.com/\">Rolf Olsen</a>,<br>Data - <a href=\"http://voyager.jpl.nasa.gov/\">Voyager 2</a>, <a href=\"http://pds-rings.seti.org/voyager/iss/raw_images.html\">NASA Planetary Data System</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Cruising through the outer solar system, the Voyager 2 spacecraft made its closest <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/history/30-years-ago-voyager-2-explores-neptune/\">approach to Neptune</a> on August 25, 1989, the only spacecraft to visit the most distant <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_giant\">ice giant planet</a>. Based on the images recorded during its close encounter and in the following days, <a href=\"https://www.rolfolsenastrophotography.com/Astrophotography/Solar-System/i-vjMHSxz\">this inspired composited scene</a> covers the dim outer planet, largest <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-june-23-flying-past-neptunes-moon-triton/\">moon Triton</a>, and faint system of rings. From just beyond Neptune's orbit, the interplanetary perspective looks back toward the Sun, capturing the planet and Triton as thin <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=130414\">sunlit crescents</a>. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=100808\">Cirrus clouds</a> and a dark band circle Neptune's south polar region, with a cloudy vortex above the pole <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/1003.3240\">itself</a>. Parts of the <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rings_of_Neptune\">very faint ring system</a> along with the three bright ring arcs were first imaged by Voyager during the fly-by, though the faintest segments are modeled in this composited picture. Spanning 7.5 degrees, the background starfield is composed from sky survey data centered on the constellation Camelopardalis, corresponding to the outbound Voyager's view of the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/neptune/\">magnificent Neptunian system</a>. Still cruising in 2026, the Voyager 2 spacecraft is <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/where-are-voyager-1-and-voyager-2-now/\">now in interstellar space</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>Sunday's Childe\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-20-v-g-r/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nJerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261121/html"}